<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><language>en</language><title>All the news about Africa - RFI</title><description>Follow any political information, cultural, sporting and live streaming on RFI. The latest information, news and events in France, Europe and around the world.</description><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/</link><lastBuildDate>Tue, 04 Aug 2026 10:04:04 GMT</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/rss" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><category>Africa</category><title>Ghana bets on AI and coding to modernise basic education</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260804-ghana-bets-on-ai-and-coding-to-modernise-basic-education-africa-technology</link><description>
Ghana has introduced artificial intelligence, coding and computer programming into its national curriculum, for pupils from kindergarten to junior high school, becoming one of the few African countries to embed these subjects in basic education.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/c4eebdc2-8cc4-11f1-a382-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/Picture-3-7.jpeg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/c4eebdc2-8cc4-11f1-a382-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/Picture-3-7.jpeg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">dc6d6782-8cc4-11f1-b4ed-dd19f77b313a</guid><pubDate>Tue, 04 Aug 2026 09:01:49 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/c4eebdc2-8cc4-11f1-a382-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/Picture-3-7.jpeg">© Michael Sarpong Mfum</source><dc:creator>Michael Sarpong Mfum in Ghana</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Cameroon government says President Biya's prolonged absence no cause for concern</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260803-cameroon-government-says-president-biya-s-prolonged-absence-no-cause-for-concern</link><description>
President Paul Biya left Cameroon in early June for what his office described as a "short private stay in Europe." Addressing the concerns regarding this prolonged absence, a government spokesperson has told RFI the 93-year-old head of state is healthy and hard at work, even while abroad. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/46322daa-8ef1-11f1-92ec-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000-A7X96PA.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/46322daa-8ef1-11f1-92ec-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000-A7X96PA.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">43f082b6-8f29-11f1-a565-b12c1aa1cb82</guid><pubDate>Mon, 03 Aug 2026 13:58:32 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/46322daa-8ef1-11f1-92ec-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000-A7X96PA.jpg">AFP - ALBERTO PIZZOLI</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Benin’s new Senate raises questions over power and legitimacy</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260803-benin-s-new-senate-raises-questions-over-power-and-legitimacy</link><description>
Benin’s new senate was inaugurated in Porto-Novo last week, creating a second chamber of parliament with powers to review legislation, protect constitutional rules and discipline political figures.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/fcfc79a8-d44a-11f0-b461-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/2025-12-08T110950Z-9433312-RC29CIAHRLSM-RTRMADP-3-BENIN-SECURITY.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/fcfc79a8-d44a-11f0-b461-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/2025-12-08T110950Z-9433312-RC29CIAHRLSM-RTRMADP-3-BENIN-SECURITY.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3a265bb6-8bf5-11f1-a2cf-377d54a72fad</guid><pubDate>Mon, 03 Aug 2026 13:32:26 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/fcfc79a8-d44a-11f0-b461-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/2025-12-08T110950Z-9433312-RC29CIAHRLSM-RTRMADP-3-BENIN-SECURITY.jpg">REUTERS - Charles Placide Tossou</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Hundreds flee Ethiopia to war-torn Sudan following new fighting in Tigray</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260803-hundreds-flee-ethiopia-to-war-torn-sudan-following-new-fighting-in-tigray</link><description>
In Ethiopia, hundreds of people have started to flee to Sudan following Saturday's fighting, when clashes in Tigray pitted federal forces against rebels from the Tigray People's Liberation Front (TPLF). Both sides accuse each other of seeking to trigger a new conflict. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a6327e60-8f20-11f1-a0a4-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/tigray-2026-afp-000-A26B6PB.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a6327e60-8f20-11f1-a0a4-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/tigray-2026-afp-000-A26B6PB.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">b34f8102-8f1f-11f1-90aa-db88ccb4d9a8</guid><pubDate>Mon, 03 Aug 2026 12:01:13 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a6327e60-8f20-11f1-a0a4-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/tigray-2026-afp-000-A26B6PB.jpg">AFP - ABEL GEREZGIHER</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Sudan army's drone attack on court kills 35 in Darfur: NGO</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260803-sudan-army-s-drone-attack-on-court-kills-35-in-darfur-ngo</link><description>
A Sudanese rights group reported that the Sudanese army (SAF) struck a traditional court in a Rapid Support Forces-held village in North Darfur with drones on Sunday, killing 35 people.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/556b7ef0-374b-11f1-9147-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000-824J2WE-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/556b7ef0-374b-11f1-9147-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000-824J2WE-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">9e8adaec-8f19-11f1-97a7-458f4247222d</guid><pubDate>Mon, 03 Aug 2026 09:56:01 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/556b7ef0-374b-11f1-9147-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000-824J2WE-1.jpg">© AFP</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>'Marriage is no longer a shield': new law in Mauritius recognises marital rape</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260802-marriage-is-no-longer-a-shield-new-law-in-mauritius-recognises-marital-rape</link><description>
Mauritius's parliament has unanimously voted to criminalise marital rape and impose harsher sentences for femicide, after a spate of violent attacks against women exposed serious gaps in protection. New reforms aim to improve safeguards for victims who have long gone unheard and unprotected.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/dbab2eb8-893d-11f1-9204-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Kenya-Femicide-Protests.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/dbab2eb8-893d-11f1-9204-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Kenya-Femicide-Protests.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">38f8cfaa-893c-11f1-9081-45f14d3c8b2f</guid><pubDate>Sun, 02 Aug 2026 07:31:58 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/dbab2eb8-893d-11f1-9204-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Kenya-Femicide-Protests.jpg">© AP/Brian Inganga</source><dc:creator>Zeenat Hansrod</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Burkina Faso added to human rights watchlist over crackdown on civil society</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260801-burkina-faso-added-to-human-rights-watchlist-over-crackdown-on-civil-society</link><description>
Burkina Faso has been added to the watchlist of human rights monitoring NGO Civicus, with the country's military authorities drastically escalating restrictions on civil society and intensifying reprisals against its critics in recent months.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a43a6e84-80ce-11f1-a332-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Burkina-Faso-Traore.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a43a6e84-80ce-11f1-a332-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Burkina-Faso-Traore.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">617e717a-8b33-11f1-891d-f1977c9499c6</guid><pubDate>Sat, 01 Aug 2026 11:48:37 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a43a6e84-80ce-11f1-a332-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Burkina-Faso-Traore.jpg">AP - Stanislav Krasilnikov</source><dc:creator>Melissa Chemam</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Africa's top cocoa producers pledge fairer prices for farmers</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260801-africa-s-top-cocoa-producers-pledge-fairer-prices-for-farmers</link><description>
Ghana and Côte d'Ivoire, the world's two largest cocoa producers, have signed a joint declaration pledging closer cooperation on pricing, local processing, environmental protection and scientific research, as the two countries seek to secure better incomes for millions of cocoa farmers
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0e21b5d6-8cf6-11f1-a4b0-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Picture-1-1-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0e21b5d6-8cf6-11f1-a4b0-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Picture-1-1-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">d5be2b2c-8cf8-11f1-95ed-d172a205da8e</guid><pubDate>Sat, 01 Aug 2026 10:13:09 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0e21b5d6-8cf6-11f1-a4b0-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Picture-1-1-1.jpg">© Presidency of Ghana</source><dc:creator>Michael Sarpong Mfum in Ghana</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Ugandan opposition leader Besigye unconscious in ICU after collapsing in court</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260731-ugandan-opposition-leader-besigye-unconscious-in-icu-after-collapsing-in-court</link><description>
Detained Ugandan opposition leader Kizza Besigye, 70, has been left unconscious in intensive care after collapsing in a Kampala courtroom on Wednesday, his wife said, as he stands trial on treason charges.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/dbca0be0-7e74-11f1-981d-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25363825111841.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/dbca0be0-7e74-11f1-981d-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25363825111841.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3edac638-8bf5-11f1-8511-377d54a72fad</guid><pubDate>Fri, 31 Jul 2026 20:52:26 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/dbca0be0-7e74-11f1-981d-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25363825111841.jpg">AP - Hajarah Nalwadda</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Oil pollution in Nigeria: NGOs accuse Shell of bad pipeline maintenance</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260731-oil-pollution-in-nigeria-ngos-accuse-shell-of-bad-pipeline-maintenance</link><description>
After decades of operations in the Niger Delta, oil giant Shell is once again facing allegations regarding pollution. A consortium of NGOs is accusing the company of having been aware of oil spills yet allowing them to continue, in order to keep raking in ever-greater profits – amounting to nearly €16 billion in 2025.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/d14006ae-8b33-11f1-a9a5-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25044364885119.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/d14006ae-8b33-11f1-a9a5-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25044364885119.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">958cc0fc-8c05-11f1-9945-e58b7addb2db</guid><pubDate>Fri, 31 Jul 2026 20:33:58 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/d14006ae-8b33-11f1-a9a5-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25044364885119.jpg">AP - Kirsty Wigglesworth</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>In flood-prone Malawi, bananas become a shield against erosion</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260731-in-flood-prone-malawi-bananas-become-a-shield-against-erosion</link><description>
When Davie Kampira, 34, a farmer in Nsanje, southern Malawi, received 34 banana suckers in 2024, he did not envisage that the few dozen plants he tried out on a run-down piece of land, previously used for brickmaking, would transform into a small estate. Today, two years later, the land beside the river has produced nine hectares of different banana varieties, along with houses, a shop and a better life for his two children and wife.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/6a328a16-8cd9-11f1-bd6e-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Enerst-Kambiya-from-Concern-Worldwide-appreciates-Kampira-s-farm.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/6a328a16-8cd9-11f1-bd6e-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Enerst-Kambiya-from-Concern-Worldwide-appreciates-Kampira-s-farm.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">c51703ee-8cc0-11f1-a656-2db2fff5973b</guid><pubDate>Fri, 31 Jul 2026 20:29:18 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/6a328a16-8cd9-11f1-bd6e-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Enerst-Kambiya-from-Concern-Worldwide-appreciates-Kampira-s-farm.jpg">© RFI/Charles ,Pensulo</source><dc:creator>Charles Pensulo in Malawi</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>DRC court clears path to change a constitution that says it can't be changed</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260730-drc-court-clears-path-to-change-a-constitution-that-says-it-can-t-be-changed</link><description>
The Democratic Republic of Congo's Constitutional Court has approved a referendum law that critics say could pave the way for President Felix Tshisekedi to seek a third term in office, prompting a coalition of opposition parties to reject the ruling and vow to fight the change.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/9988064a-8b41-11f1-87f3-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/FotoJet-74.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/9988064a-8b41-11f1-87f3-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/FotoJet-74.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">c8a9bce2-8bfb-11f1-afa4-e32b91ea1800</guid><pubDate>Thu, 30 Jul 2026 14:07:03 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/9988064a-8b41-11f1-87f3-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/FotoJet-74.jpg"><![CDATA[© Photos AFP & AP - Montage RFI]]></source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Migrant exodus leaves South African employers short-handed</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260730-migrant-exodus-leaves-south-african-employers-short-handed</link><description>
South Africa is suffering a drop in its workforce, following the departure of thousands of foreign workers in the wake of a wave of xenophobic attacks, anti-migrant protests and intensified immigration enforcement.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/02949208-8b3c-11f1-ad9b-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/Zimbabweans-leaving-SA-afp-july-2026-000-B9WF6Y3.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/02949208-8b3c-11f1-ad9b-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/Zimbabweans-leaving-SA-afp-july-2026-000-B9WF6Y3.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">79de796a-8b31-11f1-be81-9b02f149194f</guid><pubDate>Thu, 30 Jul 2026 08:18:59 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/02949208-8b3c-11f1-ad9b-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/Zimbabweans-leaving-SA-afp-july-2026-000-B9WF6Y3.jpg">AFP - ZINYANGE AUNTONY</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Uganda health minister declares end to latest Ebola outbreak</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260729-uganda-declares-end-to-latest-ebola-outbreak-on-its-soil</link><description>
Uganda's health minister on Tuesday declared the country Ebola-free, following the latest outbreak of the viral haemorrhagic disease which infected 20 people and killed two in the country.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/f4cd4230-638d-11f1-9da1-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-08T152058Z-1723229578-RC2IPLAHRTG4-RTRMADP-3-HEALTH-EBOLA-UGANDA.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/f4cd4230-638d-11f1-9da1-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-08T152058Z-1723229578-RC2IPLAHRTG4-RTRMADP-3-HEALTH-EBOLA-UGANDA.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">84d0caee-8a87-11f1-bfd4-3d2e56de2bfc</guid><pubDate>Wed, 29 Jul 2026 09:39:36 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/f4cd4230-638d-11f1-9da1-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-08T152058Z-1723229578-RC2IPLAHRTG4-RTRMADP-3-HEALTH-EBOLA-UGANDA.jpg">© Abubaker Lubowa / REUTERS</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Senegal's Faye pays first visit to Mali since appointment as Ecowas president</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260728-senegal-s-diomaye-faye-on-a-visit-to-mali-his-first-as-ecowas-president</link><description>
Senegalese President Bassirou Diomaye Faye was in Bamako on Tuesday for a one-day working visit – in a dual capacity, as both head of state of Senegal and current chair of Ecowas. He is scheduled to hold talks with his Malian counterpart, Assimi Goïta, just days after launching his own political party in Dakar.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/429f778e-21ce-11ef-8af9-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/2024-05-30T204719Z_1066833909_RC2518ALCMU6_RTRMADP_3_WESTAFRICA-POLITICS.JPG"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/429f778e-21ce-11ef-8af9-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/2024-05-30T204719Z_1066833909_RC2518ALCMU6_RTRMADP_3_WESTAFRICA-POLITICS.JPG" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">193c8234-8a71-11f1-aaeb-0331a7041faa</guid><pubDate>Tue, 28 Jul 2026 14:42:05 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/429f778e-21ce-11ef-8af9-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/2024-05-30T204719Z_1066833909_RC2518ALCMU6_RTRMADP_3_WESTAFRICA-POLITICS.JPG">via REUTERS - Senegalese Presidency</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Senegal's Faye breaks ties with Sonko to launch new political party</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260728-diomaye-faye-breaks-with-sonko-launches-new-party-in-senegal</link><description>
Senegal's President Bassirou Diomaye Faye on Saturday launched a new political party called Kiiraay, following his high-profile falling-out with his former mentor and ex-prime minister, Ousmane Sonko, and his party Pastef.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a329ea56-8998-11f1-9b75-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Senegal-Kiiraay-launch-Diomaye-Faye-July2026-afp-000-C37Q6F7.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a329ea56-8998-11f1-9b75-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Senegal-Kiiraay-launch-Diomaye-Faye-July2026-afp-000-C37Q6F7.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">76d84dd0-8998-11f1-ba77-9f6e96b6eca3</guid><pubDate>Tue, 28 Jul 2026 08:30:38 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a329ea56-8998-11f1-9b75-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Senegal-Kiiraay-launch-Diomaye-Faye-July2026-afp-000-C37Q6F7.jpg">AFP - SEYLLOU</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Niger’s junta marks three years amid rising violence and lost freedoms</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260727-niger-s-junta-marks-three-years-amid-rising-violence-and-lost-freedoms</link><description>
